<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Petar Dobrović</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1890">1890</a>–<a href="/years/1942">1942</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Petar Dobrović was a Serbian painter from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pecs">Pécs</a> and one of the leading modernist painters of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a>. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-trianon">Treaty of Trianon</a> returned the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baranya">Baranya</a> region to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, he was proclaimed president of the short-lived Serbian-Hungarian Baranya-Baja Republic in August 1921. After Hungarian troops took the area he settled in Yugoslavia and returned to painting.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/petar-dobrovic.jpg" alt="Petar Dobrović" width="300" height="382" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was Despotović at Serbian Wikipedia. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Petar_Dobrovi%C4%87,_Autoportret,_1932.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
